    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/egusphere-2026-2110",
    "abstract": "Abstract. The Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC) is a critical component of the climate system. Climate models have long predicted a slowing of the AMOC due to anthropogenic forcing, with a risk of passing tipping points in the future. The question of whether the AMOC is already weakening in response to global warming is still somewhat controversial. Continuous monitoring is only available for the past two decades, a period too short to tease apart contributions of natural variability and climate change. Reconstructions of AMOC strength going back further in time have different limitations which are debated in the literature. Here we review the state of this discussion. We conclude that the balance of multiple lines of evidence strongly supports a past and ongoing AMOC slowing in response to global warming.",
    "field": "Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation",
    "problem": "The persistent debate over whether the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC) is weakening is challenged by short direct observation records, which cannot distinguish climate change from natural variability, and the debated limitations of various longer-term proxy reconstructions, creating seemingly inconclusive evidence.",
    "method": "This opinion paper reviews and synthesizes multiple independent lines of evidence\u2014including temperature fingerprints, salinity content, and paleoclimate data\u2014to argue that the balance of evidence strongly supports a past and ongoing weakening of the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation.",
    "task": "The task is to review and synthesize multiple lines of evidence to argue that the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC) is undergoing a past and ongoing slowing in response to global warming, contrary to the perception that the evidence is inconclusive.",
